---
export const prerender = true;

import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PageIntro from "../components/PageIntro.astro";

const updated = "August 25, 2026";
---

<Layout
  title="Trust & Security"
  description="How ConvertBrio handles your files, reports of abuse or security issues, and corrections to published guides."
>
  <PageIntro eyebrow={`Last updated ${updated}`} title="Trust & Security" />

  <section class="bg-canvas">
    <div class="container-page py-16 sm:py-20">
      <article class="prose prose-neutral max-w-2xl prose-headings:font-semibold prose-headings:tracking-[-0.01em] prose-a:text-brand-600 prose-a:no-underline hover:prose-a:underline">
        <p>
          ConvertBrio is independently operated by Subham Sharma, based in India. This page
          summarizes how we handle your files, how to report abuse or a security issue, and how
          we handle corrections to published guides. For the full legal detail, see our
          <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a> and <a href="/terms">Terms of Service</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>How your files are handled</h2>
        <p>
          Files are transmitted over an encrypted (HTTPS/TLS) connection and stored encrypted at
          rest while processing. Every conversion runs as an automated process — no one manually
          opens, reviews, or reads the contents of your file at any point, including for AI tools
          like the image enhancer, upscaler, and background remover, where uploads are used only
          to produce your output and never to train or improve any model. Uploaded and converted
          files are automatically deleted within 24 hours of processing via a daily cleanup job,
          whether or not you download the result — this isn't a manual or best-effort process, it
          runs on a schedule regardless of what happens on our end.
        </p>

        <h2>Reporting abuse or misuse</h2>
        <p>
          File-upload services can be misused — to process content someone doesn't have the
          rights to, or for other abuse. If you believe a file processed through ConvertBrio
          involves illegal content, copyright infringement, or another form of misuse, report it
          via <a href="/contact">our contact form</a> or
          <a href="mailto:abuse@convertbrio.com">abuse@convertbrio.com</a>. We don't display
          uploaded filenames or results publicly, and we review abuse reports directly rather than
          through an automated queue.
        </p>
        <p>
          Tools like Unlock PDF are built only to remove owner-level restrictions from documents
          you already have the right to edit — see the
          <a href="/tools/unlock-pdf">Unlock PDF tool</a> and our guide on
          <a href="/blog/pdf-password-restrictions-explained">PDF password restrictions</a> for
          exactly what that does and doesn't cover. They're not built or intended to bypass
          protection on files you don't own or aren't authorized to modify.
        </p>

        <h2>Reporting a security issue</h2>
        <p>
          If you find a security vulnerability in ConvertBrio — anything from an access-control
          issue to a way to see another user's data — please report it to
          <a href="mailto:security@convertbrio.com">security@convertbrio.com</a> or through
          <a href="/contact">the contact form</a> before disclosing it publicly. As an
          independently operated project, response times won't match a large company's security
          team, but every report is read and taken seriously.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections to guides and articles</h2>
        <p>
          Articles on the <a href="/blog">ConvertBrio blog</a> are written to be accurate and
          practical, and are reviewed before publishing. If you spot a factual error, an outdated
          claim, or something that no longer matches how a tool actually behaves, let us know via
          <a href="/contact">the contact form</a> — corrections are made directly in the article,
          not silently, and the page's stated date reflects when it was last substantively
          updated.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
